<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> cross-country (Nordic) skiing means gliding across mostly flat or rolling terrain under your own power, with your heels free. Beginners usually start with classic technique on groomed tracks at a Nordic center, using rental skis, boots, and poles. Book a short lesson, dress lighter than you would for downhill, and expect a great workout.</p></div>

<h2>Classic vs skate skiing</h2>
<p>There are two main techniques, and they use different skis and groomed lanes.</p>
<table>
  <caption>Classic vs skate cross-country skiing compared</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Compare</th><th scope="col">Classic</th><th scope="col">Skate</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Motion</th><td>A walking or running stride with skis parallel</td><td>A side-to-side push, like ice skating</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Where</th><td>In set tracks (grooves) on groomed trails</td><td>On the wide, smooth groomed lane beside the tracks</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Learning curve</th><td>Easier to start</td><td>Harder at first, faster once learned</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Best for</th><td>Beginners, touring, relaxed days</td><td>Fitness and speed</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Most first-timers start with classic. Once you're comfortable gliding and balancing, skate skiing is a fun next step.</p>

<h2>Gear and rentals</h2>
<p>You don't need to buy anything for your first day. Nordic centers rent packages that include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Skis:</strong> long, narrow, and light. Classic skis have a grip zone under the foot (waxed or textured "waxless" pattern) so you can push off without slipping back.</li>
  <li><strong>Boots:</strong> light and flexible, more like a sneaker than a downhill boot.</li>
  <li><strong>Bindings:</strong> clip only at the toe, so your heel lifts freely.</li>
  <li><strong>Poles:</strong> longer than downhill poles, used for propulsion.</li>
</ul>
<p>Waxless classic skis are the easiest choice for beginners because they grip without any wax prep.</p>

<h2>Groomed Nordic centers vs backcountry</h2>
<h3>Nordic centers</h3>
<p>Nordic centers groom trails with set tracks and skate lanes, mark loops by difficulty, and usually offer rentals, lessons, and a warming hut. A trail pass is typically required. This is the best place to learn. Many downhill resorts also run Nordic trail networks.</p>
<h3>Backcountry and touring</h3>
<p>Touring on ungroomed trails or through the woods uses wider, sturdier skis with metal edges. It's rewarding, but it means breaking your own trail, navigating, and managing winter hazards. Build skills on groomed trails first, and take an avalanche course before skiing anywhere near steep slopes.</p>

<h2>Your first day</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Take a lesson.</strong> An hour with an instructor speeds up learning the stride, stopping, and getting up.</li>
  <li><strong>Dress lighter than for downhill.</strong> You'll warm up quickly. Wear a thin base layer, a light insulating layer, and a breathable shell you can unzip. See <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %> for the layering basics, then go a layer lighter.</li>
  <li><strong>Start on flat terrain.</strong> Practice shuffling, then gliding on one ski at a time.</li>
  <li><strong>Learn to slow down.</strong> Step out of the tracks and use a small wedge (pizza) on gentle downhills.</li>
  <li><strong>Climb with a herringbone.</strong> On steeper uphills, angle your ski tips out in a V and step up.</li>
  <li><strong>Pace yourself.</strong> Pick a short loop first; it's more tiring than it looks.</li>
  <li><strong>Bring water and a snack.</strong> Nordic skiing burns a lot of energy.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Cross-country vs downhill skiing</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Effort:</strong> cross-country is aerobic exercise the whole time; downhill uses lifts and gravity.</li>
  <li><strong>Cost:</strong> trail passes and rentals are usually much cheaper than lift tickets.</li>
  <li><strong>Speed and thrills:</strong> downhill is faster and steeper; cross-country is about rhythm and scenery.</li>
  <li><strong>Gear:</strong> Nordic gear is lighter and more comfortable to walk in.</li>
</ul>
<p>Plenty of skiers do both. If you're curious about downhill too, see <%= link_to "how to ski", guide_path("how-to-ski") %>. For more snow activities, browse <%= link_to "winter adventures beyond the lifts", guide_topic_path("winter-adventures") %>.</p>
<% faqs << { question: "Is cross-country skiing easier than downhill skiing?", answer: "It's easier to start on flat groomed trails, and falls are gentler. It's more tiring because you supply all the power, and downhill sections take practice with free-heel gear." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Should beginners learn classic or skate skiing?", answer: "Most beginners start with classic technique on groomed tracks. It's easier to learn, and skate skiing is a natural next step once you're comfortable gliding." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Do I need to buy cross-country skis to try it?", answer: "No. Nordic centers rent skis, boots, and poles, and waxless classic skis are the easiest rental option for a first day." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "What do I wear for cross-country skiing?", answer: "Dress lighter than for downhill: a moisture-wicking base layer, a light insulating layer, and a breathable shell, plus thin gloves and a hat you can stash as you warm up." } %>
